WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.480
That's not just any stunt cameraman.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.410
That's Sam Hargrave, the director

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.330
of Netflix's action movie "Extraction."

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.100
To create this one shot
required a complicated maneuver.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.880
First he unstrapped himself
from the hood of a car,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.945
then he ran over to
another car to film inside,

00:00:20.945 --> 00:00:22.710
and then he had to repeat the same shot

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.940
from two different positions precisely

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.160
to allow for a seamless final edit.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.800
The result was this: a
shot that goes smoothly

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.660
from outside a fast-moving
vehicle to inside the car

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.940
without any visible interruption.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.360
And that's just three seconds
of a 12-minute-long sequence

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.280
filmed to look like a single shot,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.200
which required camera moves
by foot and by harness,

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.790
complex stunts, invisible cuts,

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.990
and a cast that could endure
extended fight scenes.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.730
The director wanted the audience

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.560
to see the complicated act
of mercenary Tyler Rake

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.690
extracting the kidnapped
Ovi happen in real time

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.480
and felt that one long
take was the way to do it.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.210
The entire sequence
consists of a car chase,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.940
then a chase up some stairs,
a fight in a building,

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.030
a jump from one rooftop to another,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.360
a tumble off an awning,
a fight in the street,

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.550
and one final car chase,
which amounts to about

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.990
36 different segments attached together,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.250
creating an impressive 12-minute,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.400
seemingly continuous scene.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.820
From planning to shooting,
it took approximately

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.940
four to five months to bring
this one sequence to life.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.500
First they looked at large maps

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.020
of the location in India
and highlighted routes

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.870
where they would be shooting the scene.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.860
Then they choreographed the chase shots

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.060
using toy cars, labeling
each one carefully.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.610
Sam Hargrave: One of the
logistics, 'cause it's not just

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.780
me and a camera and, you know,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.050
Chris Hemsworth in a car.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.610
There's hundreds of people involved

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.747
in making this happen, so
where do you stage them?

00:01:50.747 --> 00:01:54.540
It was a really intense,
technical puzzle to solve.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.930
Narrator: Hargrave is no stranger

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.170
to big, one-take action scenes.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.140
On top of being a stuntman and coordinator

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.200
for multiple Marvel movies,
he famously choreographed

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.810
the memorable 10-minute,
one-take fight scene

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.150
in "Atomic Blonde."

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:08.790
While most movies might require

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.540
one or two location scouts,

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.530
this movie required seven to eight.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.780
During scouting, the crew needed to find

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.560
the most narrow and
claustrophobic streets possible

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.840
as well as buildings
that provided passageways

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.870
for easy, continuous shooting.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.430
Sometimes they would alter the
location to fit the sequence,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.520
and other times the location would alter

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.850
what the script looked like.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.990
For example, while this one building

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.007
offered the perfect
balcony for Chris Hemsworth

00:02:33.007 --> 00:02:35.490
and Randeep Hooda to start fighting,

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.820
it did not have an awning

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.420
that allowed their characters
to tumble off the building,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.100
so the crew constructed one.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.000
Before actually shooting the scene,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.530
all of the stunts were rehearsed
and filmed on location.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.440
Hargrave: We were testing
and proving everything

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.400
so that when we got there,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.070
there were no unanswered questions.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:54.630
We knew exactly where
stitches were gonna happen,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.140
how were we going to, you know,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.440
throw this person over a
balcony and capture that.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.970
Narrator: The sequence
beings with a car chase.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.820
This is when Hargrave strapped himself

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.240
to the hood of a vehicle.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.770
The small vehicle allowed him

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.250
to squeeze through some tight
spaces, like this tunnel.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.410
Most movies film car
chases using a pursuit arm,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.650
which is a camera mounted

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.790
to a vehicle meant to follow action.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.910
You can see one used
during the chase scenes

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.420
though the streets of
Busan in "Black Panther."

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.880
So, why do this instead?

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.870
Stylistically, it allowed them to switch

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.200
more seamlessly to a handheld
style when they needed to.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.160
Practically, it allowed for mobility

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.400
for shots where the camera
switches perspective

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.527
from outside Tyler Rake's car to inside.

00:03:36.527 --> 00:03:38.020
Hargrave's vehicle had to pull up

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.280
directly alongside Rake's car,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.610
then Hargrave unclipped himself

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.800
and put the camera as far
inside the car as possible.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.100
Then, once he could get inside the car,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.530
he leaned out the window
and then pulled the camera

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.271
back in to reshoot the same action

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:54.850
and the same background.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.390
This allowed the two shots
to be stitched together.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.350
Hargrave said that he had
to replicate it precisely,

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.530
and if he was off by even a few degrees,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.270
they would have to
shoot it all over again.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.850
Eventually, they have to
get the two main characters

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.230
out of the car and onto the street.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.150
In order to get the
camera to leave the car

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.370
without a cut, the
window needed to shatter

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.780
during this crash.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.450
The camera was hard-mounted inside the car

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.070
and then put onto a slide,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.230
where it could then be pushed

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.050
through the now empty window space

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.630
so Hargrave could grab
it and continue the shot

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.320
up a flight of stairs as
he chased a group of cops

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.880
and then eventually met back up

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.500
with the two main characters.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.290
That's where things got complicated.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:34.730
Because the hallways were only about

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.440
three and a half feet wide,

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.230
he had to avoid stepping
over fallen bodies

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.220
or running directly into the fight.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.060
And when you see the camera back up

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.100
and eventually follow Ovi away,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.540
Hargrave had to count
the exact number of steps

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.750
he was walking backwards so
he wouldn't crash into a wall.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.702
Hargrave: To get that
right, I think we had to do,

00:04:52.702 --> 00:04:55.115
I think it was 21 takes.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.140
Narrator: And because
the crew was so mobile,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.050
they had to rely on natural light

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.400
as much as possible to light scenes.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.110
Lighting kits were only
used when necessary,

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.510
like inside cars and inside the truck.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.950
While this was all one
long, continuous take,

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:15.000
they still needed to call
"Cut!" at some point.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.640
So the sequence is full
of invisible stitches,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.440
like when the window was shattered.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.950
Hargrave: Sometimes, the
places, the locations,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:23.410
weren't right next to each other

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.060
and they weren't sequentially,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.120
you weren't able to shoot

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.020
and move through them sequentially.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.540
Narrator: When Ovi ducks into a room

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.000
and starts throwing objects,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.420
that doorframe was used as a stitch point.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.690
In reality, they recreated the room

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.740
in another building down the street.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.980
Hargrave: It was, like,
you know, 10 blocks away

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.440
because the room had more access,

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.190
had more space to shoot.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.660
Narrator: If not done correctly,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.030
this can create a continuity nightmare.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.720
So to successfully cobble
these shots together,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.920
they had to recreate the first doorframe

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.430
in the second location,

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.240
so they looked exactly
the same in both places.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.740
Hargrave: That actual
location where he comes out

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.600
is another place where we had to stitch,

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.360
and that was just a couple
doors down from that location

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.468
'cause what we're trying to, you know,

00:06:07.468 --> 00:06:08.834
get the person to believe

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:10.410
is he goes in that door,
goes down the room,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.300
and comes out, you know, not far away

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.620
where Chris still was.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.430
Narrator: Stitches were also necessary

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.520
for some of the more dangerous stunts.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.860
When Tyler jumps from one roof to another,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.700
they had to switch from
Hemsworth to his stunt double.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.230
So they put in a stitch right here,

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.070
as the shot switches to the
back of the character's head,

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.530
allowing Hemsworth to
swap out with his double.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.880
Stunt doubles came in again when Hemsworth

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.740
and Hooda's characters
tumble off a balcony

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:36.907
and into the street.

00:06:38.237 --> 00:06:40.402
Director: Three, two, one, go!

00:06:40.402 --> 00:06:42.159
Narrator: Here's Hemsworth
watching as his double,

00:06:42.159 --> 00:06:44.250
Bobby Holland Hanton, took the fall.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.610
This was one of the
hardest shots to capture

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:48.800
and was as much a stunt for Hargrave

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.370
as it was for the actors.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.400
Hargrave said that,
normally, a shot like this

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.020
might have anywhere from
four to five cameras.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.130
Hargrave: Whatever they'd give
me, as many as they could,

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.890
so that you could, if something goes wrong

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.690
or if the pacing's off,
you could cut around it.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:02.200
Narrator: With just one camera...

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.910
Hargrave: Because I
wanted to be close to them

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.560
to feel the impact of the fall

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.130
but also wide enough to see the fall.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:10.210
Narrator: So to get the shot,

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.130
Hargrave jumped off the balcony

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.750
and over the awning with them,

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.500
roped into a pulley system.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.540
It allowed him to hop right
behind them as they fell

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.860
and then got on the ground right on time.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.650
After five tries, they got it right.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.840
Then the two actors
continued to duke it out

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.000
in the middle of the street.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.680
Because there are no visible cuts

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.030
and the camera had to
keep rolling and rolling,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.790
the fight scenes were especially
challenging for the actors.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.630
Because they couldn't
cut to different angles,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.830
there was no way for them to fake it.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.960
The actors would also
have to be ready to move

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:43.230
as a small vehicle goes right
between them during the fight,

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.320
a detail they incorporated
after location scouting.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.130
In the final stretch of the sequence,

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:51.890
Tyler and Ovi steal a truck
for one last big chase.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.910
They actually did use a special vehicle

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.660
with a pursuit arm for this part.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.980
This was the only way they
could physically keep up

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.170
with the one-unbroken-take illusion.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:02.500
Hargrave: We had a camera
vehicle so that we could

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.340
race up beside the truck
and get into the car

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.510
deeper than I could.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.897
When I was safety-ed in,
my arms only go so far.

00:08:08.897 --> 00:08:10.270
But we could get this action vehicle

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:11.410
in a little deeper to the car.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.330
Narrator: CGI came in handy for the moment

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:15.750
where Tyler and Ovi jump out of the truck.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.050
According to Hargrave,

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.060
the jump was originally just hinted at.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.000
However, in the editing room,

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.110
they decided they wanted
to actually show it,

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:25.600
but this location was no longer available.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.690
So first they had to rebuild the street

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.590
and tunnel on an airport
tarmac in Thailand.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:33.290
There, they had the
stunt doubles leap out.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:35.100
After that, they worked backwards

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:38.100
by having the two actual
actors recreate the moment

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.040
before the characters
jumped out of the truck

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:41.620
in front of a green screen.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.180
So this shot of them about
to leap out of the truck

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:45.940
and them actually falling out

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.360
is two different shots in two
completely different locations

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.990
connected seamlessly.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.280
When the truck flips over,
the continuous take ends.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.610
While it was important to
keep the momentum going

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.630
and always have something
exciting happening on screen,

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.310
Hargrave also found the best way

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.050
to keep the audience engaged throughout

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:04.490
was by pulling back.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.410
In the moments before Tyler
and Ovi jump off the roof,

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.800
there was supposed to be
a huge fight on the roof,

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.030
but Hargrave decided to scrap it.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.120
Hargrave: There's been
so much action before,

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.360
and there's about to be so much more.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.450
And we can't make this
decision in the editing room.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.270
We can't just, you know, cut around

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:21.590
and say, "Well, let's speed this up."

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.247
We said, "I think right
now we're overloading

00:09:24.247 --> 00:09:25.197
the audience with action.

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:26.127
We need to take a break,

00:09:26.127 --> 00:09:28.150
we need to, like, insert
a little joke here."

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:29.270
Narrator: So they settled with this

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:31.610
unscripted moment of comic relief.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.717
Tyler: All right, kid, you trust me?

00:09:32.717 --> 00:09:33.550
Ovi: No.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:34.383
Tyler: Good.

00:09:35.243 --> 00:09:36.076
Ovi: No!

